# .skillsentryignore — skillsentry auditing its own repository (R3).
#
# This repo IS a security scanner: its rule sources, test fixtures, and spec docs
# legitimately CONTAIN the very attack patterns the tool detects. Excluding them lets
# `skillsentry .` return an honest PASS on this repo. The exclusions are DISCLOSED
# in every report (transparency invariant) and `--no-ignore` re-scans everything, so
# nothing here can hide a real finding.

# Labelled malicious/benign fixture corpus — the success oracle, intentionally hostile.
tests/corpus/**

# Test files that embed attack strings as inputs to the matchers.
tests/integration/**
src/**/__tests__/**

# Rule-source files: the detection patterns themselves.
# R4 externalised the rules into declarative DATA (src/core/rules/**) plus a closed registry of
# named structural matchers (src/core/matchers/**). These files legitimately CONTAIN the attack
# patterns and per-rule fail-fixtures the tool detects, so they are excluded-and-disclosed exactly
# as the former compiled-in scanners were. The ruleset is DATA, never executed (ADR-005), so
# excluding the data files cannot hide a real finding — `--no-ignore` re-scans them and the
# exclusion is reported in every audit.
src/core/rules/**
src/core/matchers/**

# Specification and idea/opportunity docs that quote attack patterns as examples.
doc/**
ROADMAP.md

# Build output (compiled copies of the rule sources).
dist/**
